      "writtenOn": "2021-02-20T17:54:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this overall change is something we should go ahead with. The main reason is to increase the accuracy of all TCPMv2 timers from the current 5 ms error to better than 1 ms. Saving 1500 bytes of flash is a secondary benefit that I did not expect.\n\nBut I\u0027d feel much more confident that there are no unexpected changes to behavior if the changes for each timer were purely mechanical (find and replace). I think this might be possible, if the base framework is adjusted to automatically disable the timer after expiration.",
      "revId": "9b3750b292c4adc2ffe47f13c27f84cf80635785",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5f6a34aa_8529949a",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1346479
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-22T16:41:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was not expecting the flash savings either.\n\nI am concerned about changing the timer state meanings. The old code has three expected states; active (timer\u003e0 \u0026\u0026 timer!\u003dDISABLED), inactive (timer\u003d\u003d0) and disabled (timer\u003d\u003dDISABLED). That is why I coded it as I did.  If I make active become inactive because they expire, it could confuse the expected behavior.  I had thought about moving the expired code into one spot and performing this transition and at the last minute convinced myself that I was going to hit a lot of issues due to changing the state meanings.  I will double check to see if I can get it to run this way instead.\n\nThe concept change that I made makes sure that timers are not left in a non-DISABLED state when we are leaving a state machine state and that timer instance is meaningless outside of that state.  This means there is no reason to make a timer DISABLED on entry, as it is already DISABLED.\n\nAn unexpected behavior of leaving the timers as they were but using the active timers as triggers for earlier task waking includes a state that had a timeout timer running but the state accomplished its goal before the timeout and leaves. Although it only hits once, a future early task wake will happen if that timeout timer is allowed to remain active.",
      "parentUuid": "13169aec_101b1f51",
      "revId": "9b3750b292c4adc2ffe47f13c27f84cf80635785",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e76334ad_2b755122",
